  "applicability_scope": [
    "User-reported Level 1 review of every theorem declared in the current P15 Overview (1.5, 1.7, 1.8, and 1.9), plus all definitions needed to interpret their hypotheses, conclusions, and named functions.",
    "Theorem 1.5: The fixed semantic finite-scope generator eventually outputs target members fresh from the observed history, on every exact presentation of an infinite target subset.",
    "Theorem 1.7: The fixed finite-scope semi-index has an infinite denoted intersection at every round and an eventually target-contained intersection.",
    "Theorem 1.8: The concrete raw-index Algorithm-1 run gives semi-index generation and contains the whole enumerated subset at arbitrarily late rounds.",
    "Theorem 1.9: For a class of nonempty natural-number languages, causal language-valued full-text identification is equivalent to countability and the full-enumeration TD property.",
    "Natural-number languages, exact positive/partial presentations, consistency, finite scopes, intersections, sample sets, fresh choice, and the fixed finite-scope generation and semi-index predicates.",
    "The concrete Algorithm-1 named index run, recursive cursor, chain state, Rule-2 reset predicates and totalization, and infinitely-often enumerated-set containment.",
    "Causal language-valued full-text learners, ordered histories, eventual equality, countability, finite-evidence full opens, TDPoint, and TDSpace. Theorem 1.9 uses nonempty languages and does not pre-assume a countable class.",
    "Four identical normalized public/canonical statement-type pairs represent the same four source claims. Supporting proof-certificate types and generated helpers are guards only; the Supporting facade is separately hashed."
  ],
  "not_automatically_tracked": [
    "Other declarations imported from Results.Supporting, including conditional density claims, Corollaries 4.10--4.11, and the partial-text counterexample; unformalized main Theorems 1.10--1.11 and detailed 4.12--4.13.",
    "Level 2 algorithm/construction correspondence, Level 3 proof bodies or intermediate proof correspondence, and independent source-error diagnostics/counterexample proofs.",
    "Exact roundwise identity with the source compressed Algorithm 1 or three-case generator, effective computation, restricted-oracle procedures, runtime and query bounds.",
    "Other source editions, standard-library implementations/proofs, and other papers that reuse shared definitions. Existing source assessments and partial formalization coverage are retained."
  ],
